{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["The sense of <b>See, I have taught you</b>: See that I am teaching you today, as it says (Deuteronomy 11:13): <b>which I command you today</b>, and <b>which I set before you today</b> (Deuteronomy 4:8). Or it may say 'I have taught' because he mentioned doing so in the midst of the Land — that is, see, at the time you come to the Land, that I have taught you statutes and ordinances as YHWH my Elohim commanded me. Now, at first he spoke of the matter that is a general principle for everything — positive and negative commandments; and now he warns concerning the statutes, which are His decrees, and the ordinances, which are the laws, for these require strengthening more, because the reason of the statutes is hidden, and because by justice a land is established. And he said 'to do so in the midst of the Land' to warn concerning all of them, for there are many statutes and ordinances that do not apply outside the Land; or he may hint that the essence of all the commandments is in the Land, as I hinted in the secret of the Land (Leviticus 18:25). And he said that in the statutes and ordinances there are great benefits, for they are an adornment to those who do them among the nations, and even those who hate them will praise them for them. Furthermore, they are a benefit beyond compare, that HaShem will be near to them whenever they call to Him, and likewise the nations will reflect on this and know that the statutes are made with wisdom and understanding to draw near to Elohim, and they will fear them — and this is what he said: 'For what great nation is there.' Furthermore, the statutes and ordinances in themselves are righteous, upright, and good for the settlement of the people and the governance of states."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 4, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T02:45:10.927553609Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}